(Abstract) Tj
T*
(Internet-based psychological interventions show promise for childhood anxiety, but long-) Tj
T*
(term effectiveness in community settings remains understudied. We randomized 465 Finnish) Tj
T*
(children \(mean age 11 years; 71% female\) with elevated anxiety to receive either) Tj
T*
(10-session web-based therapy with clinician contact \(n=234\) or psychoeducation via digital) Tj
T*
(delivery \(n=231\). Outcomes were measured at 12 and 24 months using standardized anxiety) Tj
T*
(assessment tools. At the 12-month assessment, the intervention group demonstrated) Tj
T*
(clinically meaningful reductions in reported anxiety \(mean difference 4.6 points; 95% CI) Tj
T*
(2.3-6.9; p<0.001\). Similar benefits persisted at 24 months \(mean difference 4.3 points;) Tj
T*
(95% CI 1.8-6.8; p=0.001\). The magnitude of improvement increased from baseline to 12) Tj
T*
(months then stabilized. Subgroup analysis revealed consistent benefits across anxiety) Tj
T*
(presentations. No serious adverse events were documented. Parental mental health outcomes) Tj
T*
(did not differ between groups. Long-term engagement with the intervention was associated) Tj
T*
(with sustained symptom improvement.) Tj
